# **What Actually Happens to Your Credit When You Apply for a Mortgage**

**If You're About to Apply, Read This First**

If you're a first-time homebuyer getting ready to apply for a mortgage and you haven't thought carefully about your credit in the last few months, this article is for you. What happens to your credit score during the mortgage process is not complicated, but it surprises nearly every first-time buyer, and a few common mistakes can delay or derail a loan that should have closed without a problem.

**The Part Nobody Explains Before You Apply**

Your credit score at application is not necessarily your credit score at closing. Lenders pull your credit at the start of the process and often again just before closing. Anything that changes your score or your debt picture between those two pulls can affect your loan terms, your rate, or your ability to close at all. Most first-time buyers don't know a second pull is coming. That's the piece that creates problems.

---

**Why Normal Financial Behavior Can Trigger Underwriting Issues**

Here's where buyers get caught. Between application and closing, the instinct is to keep living normally. Buy furniture for the new house. Finance a washer and dryer. Open a store credit card to get the discount on appliances. Take out a car loan because yours is aging. Every one of those moves changes your credit profile, and your lender will see it.

A new credit account lowers your average account age and creates a hard inquiry, both of which reduce your score. A new monthly payment raises your debt-to-income ratio, which can push you above the lender's threshold. Neither of those outcomes requires a dramatic financial decision. A $2,000 furniture purchase on a new card at the wrong moment has caused closings to collapse.

The mortgage process has a window, typically 30 to 60 days from application to closing, during which your financial life needs to stay as stable as possible. That's not intuitive for buyers who are simultaneously trying to prepare for a move.

---

**How Credit Inquiries and Score Changes Actually Work During the Mortgage Process**

When you apply for a mortgage, the lender performs a hard credit inquiry, which typically causes a small, temporary score reduction. If you're shopping multiple lenders, credit bureaus treat multiple mortgage inquiries within a short window, generally 14 to 45 days depending on the scoring model, as a single inquiry. Shopping around within that window protects your score. Spacing out applications over several months does not.

Your score matters beyond just qualifying. Most lenders use tiered pricing, meaning your interest rate is directly tied to your credit score at the time of rate lock. Dropping from one scoring tier to the next, even by a small number of points, can mean a meaningfully higher rate on a 30-year loan. The cost of that difference compounds over time.

The behaviors most likely to cause score movement during the mortgage process: opening new credit accounts, missing any payment on any existing account, significantly increasing utilization on existing cards, co-signing a loan for someone else, and closing old accounts you no longer use.

---

**What to Do, In Order**

1. **Pull your own credit report before you apply.** Review all three bureaus for errors, outdated accounts, or unfamiliar activity. Disputing an error takes time. Start before you need the score, not after.  
2. **Do not open any new credit accounts from the time you start the process until after closing.** This includes store cards, personal loans, car financing, and buy-now-pay-later accounts. The timing is not worth the risk.  
3. **Keep your existing card balances low.** Credit utilization, the percentage of available credit you're using, is one of the largest factors in your score. Paying balances down before application and keeping them low through closing helps.  
4. **Make every payment on time, on every account, without exception.** A single missed payment during the mortgage process can have a significant impact on your score and your loan.  
5. **Tell your lender before you do anything financially significant.** If you need to make a large purchase, move money between accounts, or take any financial action that feels unusual, ask your loan officer first. They can tell you whether it creates a problem before it becomes one.  
6. **Expect a second credit pull near closing.** This is standard. If your financial picture has stayed stable, it's a formality. If anything has changed, it won't be.

