# **1099 Loans vs W-2 Mortgages: Why Independent Contractors Get Penalized and How to Push Back**

**For the Independent Contractor Who Earns Well but Qualifies for Less**

If you work as an independent contractor, receive 1099 income, and have been told you don't qualify for the mortgage you expected despite earning a strong income, you've encountered a system that was not designed for how you work. This article explains why that gap exists and which loan products are actually built for your income structure.

**The Core Inequity in How Mortgage Lenders Treat 1099 Income**

A W-2 employee earning a given salary has straightforward documentation. Their gross income is their gross income. A 1099 contractor earning the same amount files a Schedule C, deducts business expenses, and reports a lower net income. The mortgage lender uses the net number. Two people with the same earning power and the same ability to make a mortgage payment qualify for very different loan amounts based on how their income is classified. Conventional lending wasn't built to accommodate that difference.

---

**Why the Two-Year Average Rule Compounds the Problem**

Conventional lenders require two years of self-employment history before they'll count 1099 income toward qualification at all. A contractor who left a W-2 job 18 months ago and immediately doubled their income has no qualifying income under conventional guidelines until they've filed two years of returns showing that income.

Even after two years, the averaging rule applies. A year of lower income early in your contracting career pulls your average down even if your current earnings are significantly higher. The system measures your past without accounting for where you are now.

---

**How 1099 Loans Work Differently**

A 1099 loan is a Non-QM product that qualifies you based on your 1099 income directly rather than your net Schedule C income after deductions. Most programs use 12 to 24 months of 1099 forms to calculate qualifying income, applying the gross amount shown rather than the reduced net figure that appears on your tax return.

For contractors whose business expenses are modest relative to their gross 1099 income, this produces a substantially higher qualifying number. A contractor who grossed significant 1099 income but deducted vehicle costs, home office, equipment, and professional development will see a meaningful gap between their gross 1099 income and their Schedule C net. The 1099 loan uses the gross side of that equation.

Rates run higher than conventional mortgages, as with all Non-QM products. Factor that into your total cost comparison before you commit to a product.

---

**When a Conventional Loan Still Makes Sense**

Not every 1099 contractor needs a Non-QM product. If you have two full years of 1099 income history, your Schedule C net income after deductions is still strong enough to support the purchase price you're targeting, and your debt-to-income ratio works under conventional guidelines, a conventional loan at a lower rate is the better choice. The Non-QM premium is only worth paying when it gets you to a qualification level that conventional can't reach.

Run both scenarios before you decide. The question is whether conventional qualification is sufficient, not whether Non-QM exists as an option.

---

**What to Do, In Order**

1. **Calculate your conventional qualifying income first.** Add up your net Schedule C income from the last two years and divide by 24 for a monthly figure. Add back depreciation and depletion. That's your conventional number.  
2. **Calculate your 1099 qualifying income.** Total your gross 1099 income from the last 12 and 24 months. Divide by the number of months. Compare that figure to your conventional number.  
3. **Determine whether the gap between those two numbers changes your qualification in a meaningful way.** If conventional gets you to the purchase price you need, use it. If it doesn't, the 1099 path is worth pursuing.  
4. **Confirm you have at least 12 months of consistent 1099 income history.** Most Non-QM lenders require a minimum of 12 months, with 24 months producing better pricing and a stronger application.  
5. **Shop 1099 loan rates across at least two Non-QM lenders.** Pricing on these products varies more than conventional rates. A second quote costs nothing and can meaningfully affect your payment.

---

