# **DSCR Loan vs Bank Statement Loan: How Business Owners Should Choose When Buying Investment Property**

**For the Business Owner Who Is Also Building a Rental Portfolio**

If you own a small business and you're also trying to acquire investment property, you're operating at the intersection of two financing worlds that each have their own logic. Conventional lending evaluates your personal income documentation, which for most business owners understates what the business actually produces. What you may not know is that the two most relevant Non-QM options, DSCR loans and bank statement loans, are built around completely different qualifying frameworks, and applying to the wrong one costs you time, credit inquiries, and potentially the deal.

**The Fundamental Difference Between These Two Products**

A bank statement loan qualifies you, the borrower, based on your business income as documented through deposits. Your income supports the loan. A DSCR loan qualifies the property based on whether the rental income covers the mortgage payment at a sufficient ratio. Your income is largely irrelevant. These products operate on different underwriting logic, and the right one depends on which side of that equation is stronger in your specific situation.

---

**Why DSCR Works in Your Favor as a Business Owner**

Because DSCR underwriting doesn't touch your personal income documentation, it doesn't interact with your business financials at all. Your tax write-offs, your business structure, your Schedule C net income, none of it enters the calculation. The property either cash flows at the required ratio or it doesn't.

For a business owner who has already stretched their conventional borrowing capacity or whose documented income limits what a bank statement loan can produce, DSCR opens a parallel track. You can acquire investment properties based on their performance without those acquisitions competing with your personal income qualification for other financing needs.

---

**When Bank Statement Loans Make More Sense for Investment Property**

A bank statement loan becomes the stronger choice when the property's rent-to-payment ratio doesn't meet DSCR thresholds. Not every investment property cash flows well enough to qualify under DSCR guidelines, particularly at the higher rates Non-QM products carry. A property with thin rental margins relative to its purchase price may fall below the coverage ratio most DSCR lenders require.

If your business bank deposits support the qualification through a bank statement approach, that becomes your path to the property. The tradeoff is that a bank statement loan adds the investment property debt to your personal debt-to-income calculation. Each property you finance this way uses more of your borrowing capacity. DSCR loans don't work that way, which is why investors building larger portfolios often prefer DSCR even when bank statement qualification is available.

---

**What to Do, In Order**

1. **Calculate the target property's DSCR at current Non-QM rates before you choose a product.** Divide estimated monthly gross rent by the projected PITIA. If the result is 1.10 or higher, DSCR is available at standard pricing. If it's below that, bank statement is likely your path.  
2. **Calculate your bank statement qualifying income as a baseline.** Even if DSCR works for this property, knowing your bank statement number tells you what your borrowing capacity looks like on the income-based side.  
3. **Consider your portfolio growth plans before you choose.** If you intend to acquire multiple properties over the next several years, DSCR preserves your income-based borrowing capacity for other needs. Bank statement loans consume it with each acquisition.  
4. **Check whether LLC ownership matters to your strategy.** DSCR lenders are generally more flexible about entity vesting. If acquiring investment properties in an LLC is part of your plan, DSCR accommodates that more cleanly than most bank statement programs.  
5. **Apply to one product, the right one, rather than testing both.** Every hard credit inquiry has a cost. Do the analysis before anyone pulls your credit.

