**What Multiple-Client Income Looks Like to an Underwriter**

A conventional underwriter reviewing a consultant's application isn't troubled by income from multiple clients in principle. What they're evaluating is whether that income is stable, documented, and likely to continue. Multiple clients can support that case more effectively than a single client, because income that isn't dependent on any one relationship is less vulnerable to a single loss. A consultant who earned consistent income across several clients over two years is demonstrating a more resilient income structure than one who relied entirely on a single contract.

---

**The Documentation Challenge With Multiple 1099 Sources**

The complication with multiple-client income isn't the number of clients. It's the documentation burden of accounting for all of them clearly. Each client relationship that produced income needs to be traceable through your tax returns and, depending on the loan product, your bank statements or 1099 forms. Income that appears in your bank account but can't be traced to a documented source creates underwriting questions.

For consultants who invoice clients, maintain separate business accounts, and file their taxes cleanly, this documentation is usually straightforward to assemble. For consultants who mix business and personal accounts, receive payments through multiple platforms, or have income sources that don't generate 1099s because individual payments fall below the reporting threshold, the picture can be harder to present clearly. The goal is to make your income history legible to someone reading your financial documents for the first time.

---

**How Loan Products Handle Multiple-Client Income Differently**

Under conventional guidelines, all self-employment income from Schedule C is combined and averaged regardless of how many clients contributed to it. The total net income figure is what matters.

Under a 1099 loan, lenders typically want to see 1099 forms from your clients and will use the gross income shown across all of them to calculate qualifying income. Multiple 1099s from different clients are not a problem as long as they're consistent over the qualifying period.

Under a bank statement loan, the source of individual deposits matters less than the consistency and total volume of the deposit history. A consultant who receives payments from multiple clients into a single business account will show a deposit history that reflects all of those relationships without requiring each one to be documented separately.

---

