# **What Happens to Your Mortgage Qualification When You Switch From W-2 to Independent Contractor Work**

**For the Contractor Who Made the Switch and Now Wants to Buy**

If you recently left salaried employment to work as an independent contractor or consultant and you're planning to buy a home, you've entered one of the more complicated windows in mortgage qualification. Your income may be higher than it was as an employee. Your ability to qualify for a mortgage may be significantly lower, at least temporarily. Understanding why that gap exists and how long it lasts is the starting point for navigating it.

**The Qualification Gap Nobody Warns You About**

When you leave W-2 employment, you leave behind the income documentation structure that conventional mortgage lending is built around. Your pay stubs, your employer verification, your consistent monthly deposits from a single source, all of it gets replaced by contractor invoices, variable payments, and a self-employment income calculation that requires two years of tax returns to fully establish. Until you have those two years filed, conventional lenders have limited ability to count your new income toward qualification.

---

**The Specific Problem With the Transition Period**

The two-year self-employment requirement is measured from your most recently filed tax return. If you made the switch mid-year and haven't yet filed a return showing contractor income, you may have zero qualifying self-employment income under conventional guidelines regardless of what you've been earning since the transition.

If you filed one return showing contractor income, you have one year of documented self-employment history. Most conventional lenders require two. During this window, your previous W-2 income is no longer usable in most cases because it doesn't reflect your current employment status. Your new contractor income isn't fully usable yet because it lacks the required history. For many recent switchers, this creates a period of reduced mortgage eligibility that can last one to two years after the transition.

---

**How to Navigate the Transition Window**

Several Non-QM products have shorter seasoning requirements that can bridge the gap.

A 1099 loan may be available with as little as 12 months of documented contractor income. If you've completed one full year of contracting and can show consistent 1099 income over that period, some lenders will qualify you on that history alone.

A bank statement loan requires a seasoning period as well, but some programs will work with 12 months of business bank statements if the deposit history is strong and consistent. If your contracting income has been flowing into a dedicated business account since you made the switch, that account history may be sufficient for certain Non-QM programs.

If you haven't yet made the transition but are planning to, timing your home purchase strategically relative to your transition date can meaningfully shorten the waiting period.

---

**What to Do, In Order**

1. **Identify exactly where you are in the self-employment timeline.** Count the months since your first contractor income was received and determine whether you've filed any tax returns showing that income. That tells you which products are currently available to you.  
2. **If you're still within the first 12 months of contracting, consider whether waiting until you have 12 months of documented history improves your options.** A few additional months of documented income can be the difference between a product being available or not.  
3. **If you're planning the transition and haven't made it yet, consider your mortgage timeline before you leave your W-2 position.** If you intend to buy within the next two years, buying before you make the switch while your W-2 income is still fully documentable may be significantly simpler.  
4. **Open a dedicated business bank account the day you start contracting.** Every payment you receive as a contractor should flow through that account from the beginning. The cleaner and longer that deposit history is, the more useful it becomes for Non-QM qualification.  
5. **Track your 1099 income carefully from the start.** Keep records of every client, every invoice, and every payment. Documentation organized from the beginning is significantly easier to present to a lender than records assembled retroactively.

