**Why Preparation Matters More for Contractors Than for Anyone Else**

A W-2 employee preparing to buy a home needs to save a down payment, maintain their credit, and show up with pay stubs. Their income documentation is generated automatically by their employer. A contractor's income documentation is a function of decisions made months and years before the application, how income was reported, how expenses were deducted, which accounts it flowed through, and what the resulting tax returns and bank statements show. Those decisions are largely irreversible once filed. Preparation before filing is where the leverage is.

---

**The Tax Filing Decision That Affects Your Mortgage More Than Any Other**

The deductions you take on your Schedule C in the years before your mortgage application directly determine your conventional qualifying income. Every legitimate deduction that reduces your taxable income also reduces what a conventional lender will count toward your qualification.

This is not an argument against taking legitimate deductions. It is an argument for understanding the tradeoff before you file. In the one to two tax years before your purchase, a conversation with your CPA about which deductions to take and which to defer or forgo can meaningfully improve your qualifying income without exposing you to unnecessary tax liability. That conversation is worth having well before the return is filed, not after.

---

**The Bank Account Structure That Makes Qualification Cleaner**

Many contractors run their finances through a mix of personal and business accounts, accepting payments wherever they land and moving money between accounts as needed. That approach is financially functional but creates a documentation challenge when it comes time to apply for a mortgage.

A dedicated business checking account that receives all contractor income and from which all business expenses are paid produces a clean, legible deposit history. A lender reviewing that account can see exactly what your business generates, what it costs to operate, and what the net cash flow looks like without reconstructing the picture from multiple fragmented sources. Starting or maintaining that structure well before your application gives you a 12 to 24 month deposit history that is straightforward to present and straightforward to underwrite.

---

**What to Do, In Order**

1. **Have a mortgage-specific conversation with your CPA at least 12 months before you plan to apply.** Review which deductions in the upcoming tax year affect your qualifying income most significantly and whether any adjustments make sense given your purchase timeline.  
